n images formed by the mirror is:"}, "options": [{"id": "6872fc", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>40</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b1e6"}, {"id": "7da769", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>60</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b1e7"}, {"id": "6c10b7", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>160</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b1e8"}, {"id": "556ced", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>100</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b1e9"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b1ea", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4756"], "concepts": ["60e56768499d1e49588f8eda"], "question": {"text": "Light travels in two media <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> with speeds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>8</mn></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>8</mn></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math> respectively. The critical angle between them is"}, "options": [{"id": "c7c4ca", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>tan</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mfenced><mfrac><mn>3</mn><msqrt><mn>7</mn></msqrt></mfrac></mfenced></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b1eb"}, {"id": "6daa6c", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>tan</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mfenced><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mfenced></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b1ec"}, {"id": "a9f214", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>cos</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mfenced><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mfenced></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b1ed"}, {"id": "4253a9", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>sin</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mfenced><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mfenced></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b1ee"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b1ef", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fbdc66f9ce3f243b5677"], "concepts": ["60e56739499d1e49588f8b4f"], "question": {"text": "A ray of light <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></math> in vacuum is incident on a glass slab at angle <math><mn>60</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math> and refracted at angle <math><mn>30</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math> along <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></math> as shown in the figure. The optical path length of light ray from <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math> to <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></math> is:<br /><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2Feeb37b5e-3647-400e-bce6-b3687b95d31f-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 300px; height: 212px;\" />"}, "options": [{"id": "51804e", "text": "<math><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b1f0"}, {"id": "d359eb", "text": "<math><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b1f1"}, {"id": "18a77e", "text": "<math><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b1f2"}, {"id": "2b7bfa", "text": "<math><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b1f3"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b1f4", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4755"], "concepts": ["60e5678c499d1e49588f9250"], "question": {"text": "The radii of curvature for a thin convex lens are 10 cm and 15 cm respectively. The focal length of the lens is 12 cm. The refractive index of the lens material is"}, "options": [{"id": "67fbd4", "text": "1.2", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b1f5"}, {"id": "1f6049", "text": "1.4", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b1f6"}, {"id": "6b168d", "text": "1.5", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b1f7"}, {"id": "455b11", "text": "1.8", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b1f8"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b1f9", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60da9b4f62f042332c538326"], "concepts": ["60e5678c499d1e49588f925c"], "question": {"text": "A prism of refractive index&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#956;</mi></math> and angle of prism&nbsp;<em><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math></em> is placed in the position of minimum angle of deviation. If minimum angle of deviation is also <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math>, then in terms of refractive,"}, "options": [{"id": "73b929", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>cos</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mfenced><mfrac><mi>&#956;</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mfenced></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b1fa"}, {"id": "1d34a3", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>sin</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mfenced><mfrac><mi>&#956;</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mfenced></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b1fb"}, {"id": "fb8270", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>sin</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mfenced><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#956;</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msqrt></mfenced></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b1fc"}, {"id": "5a7c0a", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>cos</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mfenced><mfrac><mi>&#956;</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mfenced></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b1fd"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b1fe", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b474f"], "concepts": ["60da9b4f62f042332c5382ed"], "question": {"text": "Diameter of the objective lens of a telescope is <math><mn>250</mn><mi>&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>.</mo></math> For light of wavelength <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>600</mn><mi>&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> coming from a distant object, the limit of resolution of the telescope is close to:"}, "options": [{"id": "e908f5", "text": "<math><mn>1.5</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></msup><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b1ff"}, {"id": "e130c2", "text": "<math><mn>3.0</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></msup><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b200"}, {"id": "e6ed60", "text": "<math><mn>2.0</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></msup><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b201"}, {"id": "128224", "text": "<math><mn>4.5</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></msup><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b202"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b203", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f3b"], "concepts": ["6162fb466ed13040ec84d05e"], "question": {"text": "The incident ray, reflected ray and the outward drawn normal are denoted by the unitvectors <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mover><mi>a</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover><mi>b</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></mover></math>and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mover><mi>c</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></mover></math> respectively. Then choose the correct relation for these vectors."}, "options": [{"id": "3b27f0", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mover><mi>b</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mover><mi>a</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mover><mi>c</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></mover></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b204"}, {"id": "5a1c3e", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mover><mi>b</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mover><mi>a</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mover><mi>c</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></mover></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b205"}, {"id": "e5a9c7", "text": "<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mover><mi>b</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mover><mi>a</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></mover><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn><mfenced><mrow><mover><mi>a</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></mover><mo>&#183;</mo><mover><mi>c</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></mover></mrow></mfenced><mover><mi>c</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></mover></math></p>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b206"}, {"id": "e7dc5f", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mover><mi>b</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mover><mi>a</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></mover><mo>-</mo><mover><mi>c</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></mover></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b207"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b208", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fb9d66f9ce3f243b52e1"], "concepts": ["60e2fb9d66f9ce3f243b52a3"], "question": {"text": "The thickness at the centre of a plano convex lens is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mm</mi></math> and the diameter is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi><mo>.</mo></math>&nbsp;If the speed of light in the material of the lens is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>8</mn></msup><msup><mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>.</mo></math> The focal length of the lens is ___________."}, "options": [{"id": "5a16f3", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>30</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b209"}, {"id": "22e527", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>15</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b20a"}, {"id": "cb6ad4", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b20b"}, {"id": "82bcbb", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>30</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b20c"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b20d", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4755"], "concepts": ["6162fb4c6ed13040ec84d408"], "question": {"text": "An effective power of a combination of 5 identical convex lenses which are kept in contact along the principal axis is $25 \\mathrm{D}$. Focal length of each of the convex lens is :"}, "options": [{"id": "2c90c6", "text": "$20 \\mathrm{~cm}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b20e"}, {"id": "767148", "text": "$50 \\mathrm{~cm}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b20f"}, {"id": "c4639b", "text": "$500 \\mathrm{~cm}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b210"}, {"id": "29a3ac", "text": "$25 \\mathrm{~cm}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b211"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b212", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f39"], "concepts": ["60e5671a499d1e49588f87c2"], "question": {"text": "An object is gradually moving away from the focal point of a concave mirror along the axis of the mirror. The graphical representation of the magnitude of linear magnification <math><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></mrow></mfenced></math> versus distance of the object from the mirror <math><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi></mrow></mfenced></math> is correctly given by<br />(Graphs are drawn schematically and are not to scale)"}, "options": [{"id": "081505", "text": "<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2Ff6c4da56-e89d-43a5-b7ac-22f337115136.png?alt=media\">", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b213"}, {"id": "d6b001", "text": "<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2Fb7b0a5f8-6867-4da6-80fb-45645fbd0fac.png?alt=media\">", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b214"}, {"id": "f7b286", "text": "<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2F85dc923a-8d3e-40ee-a26a-4ba17ce9e958.png?alt=media\">", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b215"}, {"id": "e78f50", "text": "<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2F53d18e6f-43a7-461d-a727-13eac3b3a390.png?alt=media\">", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b216"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b217", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fbdc66f9ce3f243b5677"], "concepts": ["60e567fa499d1e49588fa023"], "question": {"text": "Given below are two statements : one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R)<br/>Assertion (A) : Refractive index of glass is higher than that of air.<br/>Reason ( $\\mathrm{R}$) : Optical density of a medium is directly proportionate to its mass density which results in a proportionate refractive index.<br/>In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :"}, "options": [{"id": "70d7de", "text": "$(\\mathrm{A})$ is not correct but $(\\mathrm{R})$ is correct", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b218"}, {"id": "19473e", "text": "Both $(\\mathrm{A})$ and $(\\mathrm{R})$ are correct and $(\\mathrm{R})$ is the correct explanation of (A)", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b219"}, {"id": "2a9e2b", "text": "$(A)$ is correct but $(\\mathrm{R})$ is not correct", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b21a"}, {"id": "e06ca6", "text": "Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b21b"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b21c", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60da9b4f62f042332c538326"], "concepts": ["6117b5e3478ae845c43c5e04"], "question": {"text": "A thin prism $\\mathrm{P}_1$ with angle $4^{\\circ}$ made of glass having refractive index 1.54 , is combined with another thin prism $\\mathrm{P}_2$ made of glass having refractive index 1.72 to get dispersion without deviation. The angle of the prism $P_2$ in degrees is"}, "options": [{"id": "c7735d", "text": "3", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b21d"}, {"id": "9c2145", "text": "16/3", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b21e"}, {"id": "c700a5", "text": "4", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b21f"}, {"id": "094078", "text": "1.5", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b220"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b221", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4756"], "concepts": ["60e56768499d1e49588f8eda"], "question": {"text": "There is a small source of light at some depth below the surface of water (refractive index <math><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac></math> ) in a tank of large cross sectional surface area. Neglecting any reflection from the bottom and absorption by water, percentage of light that emerges out of surface is (nearly):<br />[Use the fact that surface area of a spherical cap of height <math><mi>h</mi></math> and radius of curvature <math><mi>r</mi></math> is <math><mn>2</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>h</mi></math> ]"}, "options": [{"id": "c25ddb", "text": "<math><mn>21</mn><mi>%</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b222"}, {"id": "b10bd8", "text": "<math><mn>34</mn><mi>%</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b223"}, {"id": "c51c9f", "text": "<math><mn>17</mn><mi>%</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b224"}, {"id": "c8ecdc", "text": "<math><mn>50</mn><mi>%</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b225"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b226", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b474f"], "concepts": ["60da9b4f62f042332c5382ed"], "question": {"text": "In normal adjustment, for a refracting telescope, the distance between objective and eye piece is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>30</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math>. The focal length of the objective, when the angular magnification of the telescope is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math>, will be:"}, "options": [{"id": "c542af", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>20</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b227"}, {"id": "d03a11", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>30</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b228"}, {"id": "2abbcd", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b229"}, {"id": "555324", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>15</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b22a"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b22b", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fb9d66f9ce3f243b52e1"], "concepts": ["60e2fb9d66f9ce3f243b52a3"], "question": {"text": "A thin convex lens <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi></math> (refractive index <math><mo>=</mo><mn>1.5</mn></math> ) is placed on a plane mirror <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi></math>. When a pin is placed at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math>, such that <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>18</mn><mi>&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>,</mo></math> its real inverted image is formed at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math> itself, as shown in figure. When liquid of refractive index <math><msub><mrow><mi>&#956;</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></math> is put between the lens and the mirror, the pin has to be moved to <math><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>&#39;</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo></math> such that <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#39;</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>27</mn><mtext>&#8201;</mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>,</mo></math> to get its inverted real image at <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mi>&#39;</mi></math> itself. The value of <math><msub><mrow><mi>&#956;</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></math> will be<br /><br /><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2Ff9611927-0b9d-496d-af5b-700cba9442ad-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 230px; height: 183px;\" />"}, "options": [{"id": "5b2bf5", "text": "<math><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b22c"}, {"id": "ad314f", "text": "<math><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b22d"}, {"id": "3b396a", "text": "<math><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b22e"}, {"id": "bfeb7e", "text": "<math><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b22f"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b230", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4755"], "concepts": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b30"], "question": {"text": "For the thin convex lens, the radii of curvature are at $15 \\mathrm{~cm}$ and $30 \\mathrm{~cm}$ respectively. The focal length the lens is $20 \\mathrm{~cm}$. The refractive index of the material is :"}, "options": [{"id": "d17d00", "text": "1.2", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b231"}, {"id": "dd38cf", "text": "1.8", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b232"}, {"id": "98be0a", "text": "1.5", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b233"}, {"id": "3ae908", "text": "1.4", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b234"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b235", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fbdc66f9ce3f243b5677"], "concepts": ["60e2fbdc66f9ce3f243b565d"], "question": {"text": "Time taken by light to travel in two different materials <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi></math> of refractive indices <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#956;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#956;</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></math> of same thickness is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> respectively. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>-</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></math> and the ratio of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#956;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#956;</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>2</mn></math>. Then the thickness of material, in meter is: (Given <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></math> are velocities of light in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi></math> materials respectively)."}, "options": [{"id": "e59282", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b236"}, {"id": "0e70cd", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b237"}, {"id": "ef78d7", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>10</mn></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b238"}, {"id": "b87053", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b239"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b23a", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f39"], "concepts": ["6176f3f76581241c7072b25c"], "question": {"text": "A short straight object of height <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>100</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math> lies before the central axis of a spherical mirror whose focal length has absolute value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced close=\"|\" open=\"|\"><mi>f</mi></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>40</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math>. The image of object produced by the mirror is of height <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>25</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math> and has the same orientation of the object. One may conclude from the information:"}, "options": [{"id": "b99189", "text": "Image is real, same side of convex mirror.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b23b"}, {"id": "944025", "text": "Image is virtual, opposite side of concave mirror.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b23c"}, {"id": "8c9316", "text": "Image is real, same side of concave mirror.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b23d"}, {"id": "0ae161", "text": "Image is virtual, opposite side of convex mirror.", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b23e"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b23f", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60da9b4f62f042332c538326"], "concepts": ["60e5678c499d1e49588f925c"], "question": {"text": "The refracting angle of a prism is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math> and refractive index of the material of the prism is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>cot</mi><mfenced><mfrac><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mfenced></math>. Then the angle of minimum deviation will be"}, "options": [{"id": "3a4df9", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>180</mn><mo>&#176;</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>A</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b240"}, {"id": "1906f7", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>90</mn><mo>&#176;</mo><mo>-</mo><mi>A</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b241"}, {"id": "516151", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>180</mn><mo>&#176;</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>A</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b242"}, {"id": "d104d4", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>180</mn><mo>&#176;</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>A</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b243"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b244", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4756"], "concepts": ["60e56768499d1e49588f8eda"], "question": {"text": "In figure, the optical fiber is <math><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> long and has a diameter of <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#956;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>.</mo></math> If a ray of light is incident on one end of the fiber at angle <math><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#952;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>40</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math> , the number of reflections it makes before emerging from the other end is close to:<br />(refractive index of fiber is <math><mn>1.31</mn></math> , <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><mn>40</mn><mo>&#176;</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0.64</mn></math> and <math><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>&#8289;</mo><mrow><mn>0.49</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>30</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math> .)<br /><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2Fc02eca14-3d8a-45f4-8ba5-83c423e736aa-image.png?alt=media\" />"}, "options": [{"id": "15f453", "text": "<math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mn>55000</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b245"}, {"id": "d85b62", "text": "<math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mn>57000</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b246"}, {"id": "22480d", "text": "<math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mn>45000</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b247"}, {"id": "4bf61e", "text": "<math><mn>66000</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b248"}], "questionLabels": []}], "markingScheme": {"_id": "608283e223cf4b3ad8f125e6", "title": "JEE Single Correct", "correctAttempt": 4, "incorrectAttempt": -1, "createdAt": "2021-04-09T08:45:27.797Z", "updatedAt": "2021-07-05T05:39:15.785Z", "__v": 0, "updated_by": "6042252509c5bf34e8a2f178"}, "maximumAttemptLimit": 20, "linkedQuestions": [], "createdAt": "2026-04-14T14:30:17.880Z", "updatedAt": "2026-04-14T14:30:17.880Z", "__v": 0}, "position": 0, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b292"}, {"sectionId": {"_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b28e", "title": "Physics Numerical", "sectionType": "normal", "subject": "607018ee404ae53194e73d90", "questions": [{"_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b272", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4755"], "concepts": ["61ad9fc8dedd066c316e4e20"], "question": {"text": "The distance between an object and a screen is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>100</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math>. A lens can produce real image of the object on the screen for two different positions between the screen and the object. The distance between these two positions is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>40</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math>. If the power of the lens is close to&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mn>100</mn></mfrac></mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi></math>&nbsp; where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math> is an integer, the value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math> is&nbsp;_________"}, "options": [{"id": "2885e4", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b273"}, {"id": "a0bb33", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b274"}, {"id": "b3c3ca", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b275"}, {"id": "45e065", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b276"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b277", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fb9d66f9ce3f243b52e1"], "concepts": ["60e2fb9d66f9ce3f243b52a3"], "question": {"text": "<p>An object is placed on the principal axis of convex lens of focal length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math> as shown. A plane mirror is placed on the other side of lens at a distance of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>20</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math>. The image produced by the plane mirror is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math> inside the mirror. The distance of the object from the lens is ____ <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>cm</mi></math>.</p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2F15f873a5-081b-457d-84d4-0594c357dfcd-9894770.PNG?alt=media\" style=\"width: 228px; height: 160px;\" />&nbsp;</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "c1fe78", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b278"}, {"id": "bf56dc", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b279"}, {"id": "be48fe", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b27a"}, {"id": "ae38fd", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b27b"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b27c", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60da9b4f62f042332c538326"], "concepts": ["60e5678c499d1e49588f925c"], "question": {"text": "<p>A prism of refractive index <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> and another prism of refractive index <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> are stuck together (as shown in the figure). <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> depend on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#955;</mi><mo>,</mo></math>&nbsp;the wavelength of light, according to the relation&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><msup><mi>&#955;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>45</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><msup><mi>&#955;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></math>&nbsp;</p><p>The wavelength for which rays incident at any angle on the interface <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi></math> pass through without bending at that interface will be ____&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>nm</mi><mo>.</mo></math></p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2F2fefa5b6-0510-41de-a6f8-9f1f4887a6e8-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 293px; height: 195px;\" /></p>"}, "options": [{"id": "1e2786", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b27d"}, {"id": "9fe463", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b27e"}, {"id": "5f5d67", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b27f"}, {"id": "0a5aba", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b280"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b281", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f39"], "concepts": ["60e5671a499d1e49588f87c2"], "question": {"text": "<p>An object &#39;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>O</mi></math>&#39; is placed at a distance of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>100</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math> in front of a concave mirror of radius of curvature <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>200</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math> as shown in the figure. The object starts moving towards the mirror at a speed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>. The position of the image from the mirror after <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></math> will be at _____ <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>cm</mi></math>.</p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2F4bab1fb3-8200-4cfd-8275-c0a0f08460fa-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 150px; height: 114px;\" /></p>"}, "options": [{"id": "4c00ff", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b282"}, {"id": "650ba6", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b283"}, {"id": "c55d7d", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b284"}, {"id": "9cb7b3", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b285"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b286", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fbdc66f9ce3f243b5677"], "concepts": ["60e2fbdc66f9ce3f243b565d"], "question": {"text": "A container contains a liquid with refractive index of 1.2 up to a height of 60 cm and another liquid having refractive index 1.6 is added to height H above first liquid. If viewed from above, the apparent shift in the position of bottom of container is 40 cm. The value of H is ______ cm.<br/>(Consider liquids are immisible)"}, "options": [{"id": "d518d7", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b287"}, {"id": "5a5ba5", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792ee0e063d928b288"}, {"id": "6df0ea", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f792